  now considering very seriously  uh to change their laws so that it's  identifiable as only a jewish state  whereas about 20 percent of the total  citizens are  non-jews or muslims  i just read on the news just before i  came here i turned on my ipad  and uh  prime minister netanyahu has called for  new elections  because he's tired of the moderate  i say this moderate israelis  criticizing his more conservative  government so he's called for a new  election so he can choose a more  conservative government which i think is  a step in the right direction there's an  outsider looking in but i prayed always  every night as a matter of fact that  israel can find peace with its neighbors  because i know that there's bloodshed on  both sides and horrible hatred is  increasing every day it's something  that's worthy of direct  forceful involvement by the united  states and i think neither the  palestinians nor israel are in a  position to reject  a strong move by the president of the  united states if he decides to do so  so uh president zedillo just handed me  one last set of questions  set up questions  but they're they're essentially on the  same theme okay  and they're personal  these are students asking essentially  what is it in your life  spiritual intellectual  other  that has allowed you  to become  the great global citizen that you are  the great public servant that you are  uh uh the uh  uh and and and and how  they want to draw inspiration from this  so  uh not to put you on the spot but uh  what do you what do you see as uh the  formative uh experience for you in your  life without agreeing to the premises of  your  fairness  well you know i grew up as i said in a  in a  deprived neighborhood  nobody in my father's family had been  further than 10th grade in high school  and  my daddy wanted me to go to  college it was during the great  depression years and there were only two  three colleges in america then  annapolis and west point  and i decided to go to annapolis because  of reasons i need not describe now but i  i did and i i was lucky enough  to graduate from annapolis and was a  successful naval officer then i went  back home  to plains and i began to see  this was in 1953 i began to see the  horrible  blight of racial segregation on our  black neighbors  who were still my friends and on the  white people as well  and so i became the chairman of it  of a sumpter county  education board of school board  and i decided to run for the state  senate i never had any interest in  politics before but  the public school system was threatened  to be shut down if a black student  entered the classroom then so i went to  the state senate and i began to see that  i had  opportunities and talents  to be utilized and i remember what my  high school  superintendent said she was miss julia  coleman and i quoted her by the way  in my presidential inaugural speech and  also when i got the nobel peace prize  mrs studio coleman said we must  accommodate changing times  but cling to unchanging principles  and in a fast-changing technical world  there are new ideas and new challenges  and new opportunities  and and new  new  problems that come up but there are  certain principles  that never change and i say they're  basically expressed in our great  religions and in other moral values the  most  clear secular expression  of the highest ideals of human beings  was the universal declaration of human  rights in 1948  i believe and  that expresses  the the highest  patterns that we can set for ourselves  or  anyone under us and i've tried to live  by that  but i'm i i'm still  basically inherently a peanut farmer  from georgia and i and i've had  wonderful opportunities so i've tried to  take advantage of some of those  opportunities and when i do try  something new uh it's not because i feel  a duty to do it it's because i enjoy it  you know it's it's something that's  that's different and  exciting and un  unpredictable and challenging 